Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR sits on the edge of a made bed, elbows on his knees, staring at nothing. A bike helmet hangs on the doorknob.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One time, back in high school, Nick got so pissed off at this kid named Steve — said something mean to Nick's girl. Nick went to that kid's house in the middle of the night. Can of lighter fluid. Sprayed the yard. Set it on fire.

He shakes his head, almost smiling, almost sick.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He got away with it too. Everybody's afraid of Nick. What a set of balls.

A long beat. He looks up, like the question actually worries him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? I can't even picture them in the same room.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Later. Same room. He lies back, hands laced on his chest, thinking out loud to the ceiling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma needs me. Nick needs me — so he doesn't have to think about Ma. Sionna needs me?

He almost laughs at himself.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe I just want that to be true.

He turns his head, remembering.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The other day we talked about trees turning color. She said the sugar gets caught in the leaves — like the trees are shutting down for winter. I told her, sometimes people get caught in bad situations and they change into something better. Just like leaves. She said that was beautiful.

A small, real smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nobody ever told me anything I said was beautiful before.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Mats laid out in rows. SIONNA — calm, sharp-eyed, moves through a pose with total ease. THE NARRATOR beside her, stiff, wobbling, trying.

He nearly topples. She catches his elbow without looking, steadies him.

SIONNA
We all start out stiff. The more you go, the more bendable you get.

He grunts, half a laugh, half effort.

NARRATOR
We'll see about that.

They settle into Savasana, flat on their backs, eyes closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Lie there. Close your eyes. Your brain thinks of all kinds of stuff — and then there's just peace.

INT. LIBRARY - DAY

Shelves of exercise books. THE NARRATOR crouches, pulls a yoga title, then wanders a row over into WORLD RELIGION. He picks a slim book on Buddhism, turns it over, reads the back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Attachment. Attaching yourself to people or things makes you miserable. We look for happiness outside ourselves — but happiness is how you think about things. The more you want, the more you suffer.

He sits cross-legged right there on the floor between the stacks, absorbed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Life is suffering. The cause is the search for things. But there's hope — meditate, change your mind, and you're free. Live only in the present moment.

He closes the book slowly, stares at the cover like it just said something to him personally.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to talk to Sionna about it.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR on the phone, lying across the bed, cord looped around his fingers.

NARRATOR
I want you to come over. Meet my Ma.

SIONNA (O.S.)
(through phone)
Are you sure? It's kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

NARRATOR
Hell, she probably won't even remember meeting you when she wakes up. She's real forgetful now.

A beat.

NARRATOR
What I really need to talk to you about — is some stuff I read. About Buddhism.

SIONNA (O.S.)
(through phone)
I didn't know you were interested in Buddhism.

NARRATOR
Remember when we talked about meditating? I think I want to try it.

SIONNA (O.S.)
(through phone)
You've already tried it, silly. You're meditating at the end of every yoga class. Savasana. Corpse pose.

He blinks, surprised, then grins at the ceiling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was right. I could do that anywhere. Sit in a chair. Lay on my bed. Just zone out.

INT. NARRATOR'S APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

MA sits in front of a blaring game show, blanket over her lap, eyes glassy. THE NARRATOR drops onto the couch beside her.

NARRATOR
How's it goin', Ma?

MA
(confused, squinting at him)
What? Oh — it's you.

NARRATOR
You okay, Ma?

MA
(hand pressed to her chest)
I don't know. It feels like my heart's gonna jump right out of my chest. So strange. I'm a little dizzy too.

THE NARRATOR's face goes tight with worry. He's already reaching for the phone.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON, gray-templed, reading glasses low on his nose, faces THE NARRATOR across a desk cluttered with charts.

DR. CARSON
Hello. I'm Dr. Carson. I believe I've met you before — you're the younger son.

NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. How's she doin', Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. High enough that I'm concerned about a stroke. I want her in the emergency room today. Admitted for observation. IV medication until we get her stabilized.

NARRATOR
Whoa, man. You sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
(writing on his pad)
Has she been confused lately? Chest pressure?

NARRATOR
Yeah. She ain't been actin' too normal.

Dr. Carson's pen keeps moving. THE NARRATOR stares at the pad like it's a verdict.

INT. NARRATOR'S APARTMENT - LIV
